{"step_index":1140,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:23:58Z","content":"The culprit has been captured, dissected, and eradicated.\n\nHere is the exact technical post-mortem and the fix deployed on **`th2-revproxyp-fe1`**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Root Cause: The Duplicate `Host` Header Trap\n\nWhen testing through Nginx, Apache Tomcat was returning <span style=\"color:red;font-weight:bold;\">HTTP Status 400 – Bad Request</span>.\n\nBy sniffing the raw HTTP payload sent by Nginx to Tomcat, we uncovered duplicate headers:\n```http\nHost: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\nHost: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443\nX-Forwarded-Proto: http\nX-Forwarded-Proto: https\n```\n\n* **Why Nginx did this**: In Nginx, multiple `proxy_set_header` directives with the same key inside the same block **do not overwrite** each other — they are both injected. Including `nginxconfig.io/proxy.conf` while explicitly setting `proxy_set_header Host $http_host;` caused Nginx to inject **two distinct `Host:` lines**.\n* **Why Tomcat broke**: Tomcat complies strictly with **RFC 7230 / RFC 9112 Section 5.4**:\n  > *\"A server MUST respond with a 400 (Bad Request) status code to any request message that contains more than one Host header field.\"*\n\nDirect verification against Tomcat confirmed:\n* **Duplicate `Host` header** $\\rightarrow$ <span style=\"color:red;font-weight:bold;\">HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request</span>\n* **Single clean `Host` header** $\\rightarrow$ <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">HTTP/1.1 200 OK</span> (`{\"status\":\"UP\"}`)\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Remediation Applied on `th2-revproxyp-fe1`\n\nWe updated [`/etc/nginx/sites-available/03-map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf`](file:///etc/nginx/sites-available/03-map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf):\n\n1. **Eliminated Duplicate Headers**: Removed the generic include and declared a clean, single-pass proxy header block:\n   ```nginx\n   proxy_http_version         1.1;\n   proxy_cache_bypass         $http_upgrade;\n   proxy_set_header Upgrade   $http_upgrade;\n   proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;\n\n   proxy_set_header Host              $http_host;\n   proxy_set_header X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;\n   proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n   proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;\n   proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Ssl   on;\n   proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host  $host;\n   proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Port  9443;\n   ```\n2. **Injected Full CORS Support**: Since the browser originates from port `443` and calls port `9443` via AJAX, we added CORS headers and an `OPTIONS` preflight handler:\n   ```nginx\n   add_header 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' '*' always;\n   add_header 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods' 'GET, POST, OPTIONS, PUT, DELETE' always;\n   add_header 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers' 'DNT,User-Agent,X-Requested-With,If-Modified-Since,Cache-Control,Content-Type,Range,Authorization' always;\n   add_header 'Access-Control-Expose-Headers' 'Content-Length,Content-Range' always;\n\n   if ($request_method = 'OPTIONS') {\n       add_header 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' '*' always;\n       add_header 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods' 'GET, POST, OPTIONS, PUT, DELETE' always;\n       add_header 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers' 'DNT,User-Agent,X-Requested-With,If-Modified-Since,Cache-Control,Content-Type,Range,Authorization' always;\n       add_header 'Access-Control-Max-Age' 1728000;\n       add_header 'Content-Type' 'text/plain; charset=utf-8';\n       add_header 'Content-Length' 0;\n       return 204;\n   }\n   ```\n3. **Syntax & Zero-Downtime Reload**:\n   * `sudo nginx -t` $\\rightarrow$ <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">syntax is ok / test is successful</span>\n   * `sudo systemctl reload nginx` $\\rightarrow$ <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">reloaded cleanly</span>\n\n---\n\n### 3. Live Verification\n\n* **From Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33:9443`)**:\n  ```text\n  < HTTP/2 200 \n  < content-type: application/vnd.spring-boot.actuator.v3+json\n  < access-control-allow-origin: *\n  {\"status\":\"UP\"}\n  ```\n* **OPTIONS Preflight**:\n  ```text\n  < HTTP/2 204 \n  < access-control-allow-origin: *\n  ```\n* **From Central Backend (`172.30.198.49`)**:\n  ```text\n  < HTTP/2 200 \n  < access-control-allow-origin: *\n  {\"status\":\"UP\"}\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Testing in Centreon Web\n\n* **External / WAN / 4G connection**: Click **\"Test connection to server\"** — it connects to `109.62.64.18:9443` and turns <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">green</span>.\n* **Internal Corporate Workstation (LAN/VPN)**:\n  Because the corporate firewall drops internal workstation traffic targeting its own public VIP `109.62.64.18` (lack of Hairpin NAT), make sure your workstation points to the internal DMZ IP:\n  ```text\n  172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n  ```\n  *(In your local `/etc/hosts` or `C:\\Windows\\System32\\drivers\\etc\\hosts`)*.\n  \nAll technical details are documented in the report artifact: [centreon-map-chain-report.md](file:///home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/centreon-map-chain-report.md).","thinking":"**Confirming the Nginx Fix**\n\nThe problem's dead, buried, and burned on `th2-revproxyp-fe1`. Nginx got a swift kick, CORS and the OPTIONS preflight are happily singing, and HTTP 200 smiles back. We've got our little victory, eh?\n\n\n"}
