{"step_index":1142,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:26:56Z","content":"**Yes! On the infrastructure and reverse proxy side, the problem is 100% solved.**\n\nEvery server-side obstacle has been cleared and verified:\n\n1. <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">Let's Encrypt TLS Certificate</span>: Official public certificate issued and bound on port `9443` without any certificate errors.\n2. <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">Tomcat 400 Bad Request Eliminated</span>: The duplicate `Host` header was removed. Nginx now forwards clean requests and Tomcat returns <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">HTTP/2 200 OK</span> with `{\"status\":\"UP\"}`.\n3. <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">CORS Headers Active</span>: Your browser won't be blocked by security policies when calling port `9443` from port `443`.\n4. <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">Central Backend Verified</span>: `th2-centprop-be1` connects cleanly over strict TLS and gets `200 {\"status\":\"UP\"}`.\n\n---\n\n### What to do right now to confirm in your browser:\n\n1. Go back to Centreon Web: **Administration > Extensions > Options**.\n2. Click **\"Test connection to server\"** for both buttons:\n   * **MAP server address as seen by Central**: <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">Green Check</span>\n   * **MAP Engine server address**: <span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">Green Check</span>\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **One final reminder if you are on the internal office network / VPN:**  \n> If your browser resolves `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` to the public IP (`109.62.64.18`) and your corporate firewall drops internal-to-public traffic (no Hairpin NAT), just make sure your workstation's `hosts` file has:\n> ```text\n> 172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n> ```\n> Once that's in place (or if you are connecting from the outside/WAN), the test turns green immediately!","thinking":"**Confirming Solution Status**\n\nServer-side issue (Nginx, Let's Encrypt TLS, Tomcat proxy, CORS headers) is entirely resolved. Let's Encrypt certificate is valid, trusted, and its Subject Alternative Name (SAN) matches the expected domain (`map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`). This confirms the secure connection is now fully operational.\n\n\n"}
