{"step_index":1188,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:35:09Z","content":"This command updates the system-wide **proxy bypass list** on your Debian machine.\n\nHere is the exact explanation of what it does and **why it matters**:\n\n---\n\n### 1. What the command does line by line\n\n* `sudo sed -i 's/no_proxy=\"\\(.*\\)\"/no_proxy=\"\\1,outremer-telecom.fr,.outremer-telecom.fr\"/' /etc/environment`  \n  $\\rightarrow$ Edits `/etc/environment` to append `outremer-telecom.fr` and `.outremer-telecom.fr` to the lowercase `no_proxy` variable.\n* `sudo sed -i 's/NO_PROXY=\"\\(.*\\)\"/NO_PROXY=\"\\1,outremer-telecom.fr,.outremer-telecom.fr\"/' /etc/environment`  \n  $\\rightarrow$ Does the same for uppercase `NO_PROXY`.\n* `cat /etc/environment`  \n  $\\rightarrow$ Displays the resulting file so you can visually verify the content.\n\n#### Before vs After in `/etc/environment`:\n```diff\n  http_proxy=\"http://a.samy:koby_%40miT_S%40My@172.16.75.9:3128\"\n  https_proxy=\"http://a.samy:koby_%40miT_S%40My@172.16.75.9:3128\"\n  HTTP_PROXY=\"http://a.samy:koby_%40miT_S%40My@172.16.75.9:3128\"\n  HTTPS_PROXY=\"http://a.samy:koby_%40miT_S%40My@172.16.75.9:3128\"\n- no_proxy=\".intranet.local,localhost,127.0.0.0/8,::1\"\n- NO_PROXY=\".intranet.local,localhost,127.0.0.0/8,::1\"\n+ no_proxy=\".intranet.local,localhost,127.0.0.0/8,::1,outremer-telecom.fr,.outremer-telecom.fr\"\n+ NO_PROXY=\".intranet.local,localhost,127.0.0.0/8,::1,outremer-telecom.fr,.outremer-telecom.fr\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why is this necessary? (The Forward Proxy Trap)\n\nYour Debian machine is configured to route web traffic through a corporate forward proxy (**Squid at `172.16.75.9:3128`**).\n\nWhen an application uses a forward proxy:\n1. It **does NOT** look at your local `/etc/hosts`.\n2. Instead, it delegates the entire request to Squid: `\"Squid, please connect to map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443\"`.\n3. Squid does its own public DNS lookup $\\rightarrow$ resolves `109.62.64.18` (public IP) $\\rightarrow$ connects to `109.62.64.18:9443`.\n4. The corporate firewall **drops** that packet (no Hairpin NAT), and the connection **times out**!\n\nBy adding `outremer-telecom.fr` to `no_proxy`:\n* The system bypasses the Squid proxy completely.\n* It directly consults your `/etc/hosts` (`172.30.198.33`).\n* Traffic reaches `th2-revproxyp-fe1` instantly over the internal network.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Will it work right now in Centreon Web UI?\n\n**YES!** \n\nFor your desktop browser (**Brave**), it uses GNOME's desktop proxy settings (`gsettings`), which have **already been updated** to ignore `*.outremer-telecom.fr`.\n\nYou can test it right now in Centreon Web:\n1. Open your browser on Centreon: **Administration > Extensions > Options**.\n2. Click **\"Test connection to server\"** for:\n   ```text\n   https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443\n   ```\n3. Your browser will now bypass the proxy, connect directly to `172.30.198.33:9443`, and turn **<span style=\"color:green;font-weight:bold;\">Green</span>**!","thinking":"**Interpreting the Configuration Modification**\n\nThe command modifies a system configuration file. It appears the objective is to append specific domain names to the `no_proxy` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables, likely used to exclude certain domains from proxy usage. The `sed` command performs in-place substitution within the `/etc/environment` file. Finally, it displays the file's contents after the modifications.\n\n\n"}
